1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,906
The second attempt began with a funeral and a birthday in the same week.

2
00:00:03,906 --> 00:00:16,173
The cousin of his former wife, a tall actor and a convinced optimist with an unlikely surname, came to the house, and the family had to decide who would sit where.

3
00:00:16,173 --> 00:00:19,623
“You are a saint,” his aunt said.

4
00:00:19,623 --> 00:00:21,578
He was not.

5
00:00:21,578 --> 00:00:27,277
He was a pessimist with an appointment with the dentist, and he said so.

6
00:00:27,277 --> 00:00:39,189
Her son, a boy of nine, was there with his own question about the ceremony: he wanted to know whether a family could be chosen rather than given.

7
00:00:39,189 --> 00:00:47,418
They were wed in a small ceremony in the nursery garden, and the bride carried a bunch of flowers instead of a doll.

8
00:00:47,418 --> 00:00:56,031
The bridegroom, a salesman from the next town, had a regular job and an infinite patience for children.

9
00:00:56,031 --> 00:01:04,120
“Whoever adopts a surname in this family,” the grandmother said, “adopts a history.”

10
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:10,779
The husband of his cousin was a young giant of a man who worked with cows and could lift a calf.

11
00:01:10,779 --> 00:01:32,140
“She is my grandchild,” the grandmother said, “and so are the other grandchildren.” A nasty pill, a small creature, a youngster with a blue bicycle, an ethnic dish on the table, and a bitch with three puppies in the yard: the house was full of them throughout the afternoon.

12
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:39,089
A little of something was always missing, and the head of the table never let it show.

13
00:01:39,089 --> 00:01:45,507
At the initial meeting of the family council, they were asked to say what they expected.

14
00:01:45,507 --> 00:01:50,207
“We cannot control who marries whom,” Maya said.

15
00:01:50,207 --> 00:02:06,014
“But we can see that six people are not asked to carry one person's part.” The latter remark was, however, the one that stuck, and the figure on the whiteboard was not the real figure at all.

16
00:02:06,014 --> 00:02:10,158
So the second attempt was smaller and more regular.

17
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:20,606
I myself wrote the schedule; we allowed ourselves one evening a week; a homosexual couple in the building lent us their table and their kettle.

18
00:02:20,606 --> 00:02:33,272
“Yeah,” Maya said, “so that is the great reform: a table, an honest name, and nobody pretending that a family is a fixed thing.”
